\par 
\par \tab The caster has no control over the destination of the target. The conditions at the destination may kill the target (for example, arriving in the elemental plane of fire) or merely make life difficult. This determination is left to the DM. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Wildzone*}{\b\fs24  (Conjuration/Summoning) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 2d6 turns
\par Casting Time: 1d6 rounds
\par Area of Effect: 300'x300' square
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This powerful spell creates a disruption in magical forces similar to the conditions found in wild magic regions (areas where the effects of magic have been permanently altered). This spell has only a temporary effect, although the effects of }{
\i\fs24 wildzone}{\fs24  could possibly be rendered permanent.
\par \tab The spell creates a wild magic region centered on the caster. The area of effect cannot be shaped in any way; it is }{\i\fs24 always}{\fs24  a square 300 feet long on each side (90,000 square feet).
\par \tab Within the }{\i\fs24 wildzone}{\fs24 , wild magic reigns. Any spell cast in the area of effect is automatically treated as a wild surge (see Table 2). Effects from magical items that expend charges are also treated as w
ild surges when used in the area. Other magical items function normally.
\par \tab Spells cast into the }{\i\fs24 wildzone}{\fs24  from outside the area of effect function normally, but spells cannot be cast out of the area of effect without triggering a wild surge.
\par \tab The material components are several pots of paint which must be spilled across a sheet of hammered silver worth no less than 2,000 gp.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf2 Ninth-Level Spells }{\fs24 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Chain Contingency}{\b\fs24  (Evocation) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 day/level
\par Casting Time: 2 turns
\par Area of Effect: The caster
\par Saving Throw: None 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This powerful spell is similar to the 6th-level }{\i\fs24 contingency}{\fs24  spell.  }{\i\fs24 
\par \tab Chain contingency}{\fs24  allows the caster to designate either two or three spells that will take effect automatically under a specific set of conditions
. In other words, when a set of conditions is met, the designated spells are "cast" immediately without the caster's intervention.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Chain contingency}{\fs24  must be cast together with the spells it is to trigger. The caster may choose either two spells to occur s
imultaneously or three spells to occur consecutively, one per round. Spells must be of 8th level or lower. Only the 6th-level }{\i\fs24 contingency }{\fs24 
spell may not be included. The casting time of 2 turns includes the casting of the spells to be triggered.
\par \tab Unlike the }{\i\fs24 contingency}{\fs24  spell, spells "stored" in }{\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24 
 can affect creatures other than the caster. These instructions must be carefully worded; the spell obeys the letter of its instructions and not the caster's intentions.
\par \tab In casting }{\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24 ,
 the wizard defines the conditions that will trigger the "stored" spells. This definition must be carefully worded, but may be as limiting or general as the caster desires. The caster also states the exact order, target, range, and manner in which the sto
red spells are to be cast.
\par \tab The spell has several limitations in triggering its spells. It does not have any powers of discernment; thus, an instruction to "target the highest-level enemy" is not possible. Furthermore, the conditions cannot involve a delay;
 a spell cannot be ordered to trigger "three turns after I sneeze."
\par \tab When the named conditions are met, the }{\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24  is automatically triggered. If  all specifics of casting a spell are not specified (e.g., target or area of effect), the effect is automatically centered on the caster.
\par \tab Possible triggers might include a fall from a distance greater than the caster's height, the appearance of the first beholder within 30 feet of the caster, or the wizard pointing his finger and pronouncing a specified word.
\par \tab Only one }{\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24  can be placed on the spellcaster at any one time. If a second is cast, the first }{\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24  is cancelled. It is possible to have both a }{\i\fs24 contingency}{\fs24  and a }{
\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24  operating at the same time, provided that there is no overlap in the conditions specified for triggering the two spells.
\par \tab Spells triggered by the }{\i\fs24 chain contingency}{\fs24  have a casting time of 1. If the spell is triggered under conditions that are impossible to fulfill, it fails. If one of the spells i
n a series cannot be fulfilled, the remaining spells in the series are lost. Normal conditions, including line of sight to the target, must be fulfilled. All spells originate from the caster; thus, it is not possible for a caster to }{\i\fs24 teleport}{
\fs24  and leave behind a series of }{\i\fs24 fireballs}{\fs24  to blast his enemies. In this case, the }{\i\fs24 fireballs}{\fs24  would either fail or destroy something at the caster's destination.
\par \tab The material components are (in addition to those of the companion spells) 500 gp worth of quicksilver; a gem of
 at least 1,000 gp value; an eyelash from an ogre mage, ki-rin, or similar spell-using creature; and an ivory statuette of the wizard (which is not destroyed in the casting of the spell) which must be carried by the spellcaster in order for the }{\i\fs24 
chain contingency}{\fs24  to perform its function when triggered. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Elemental Aura}{\b\fs24  (Abjuration, Evocation) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Component: V
\par Duration: 1 hour/level
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: The caster
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell has four very different effects depending on the type (air, earth, fire, water) of }{\i\fs24 elemental aura}{\fs24  cast. Only the caster may receive an elemental aura, and it is not possible to benefit from more than one aura at one time.

\par \tab Each aura is three inches thick and covers the caster's entire body. An aura of air is hazy white in color, an aura of earth is dull grey, an aura of fire is flickering red, and an aura of water is shimmering blue. The auras have these effects: 

\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf2 Air}{\fs24\cf2 
\par \bullet }{\fs24  immunity to gas and air-based attacks 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  total protection from physical attacks by creatures of the elemental plane of Air 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to cast }{\i\fs24 fly}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 protection from normal missiles}{\fs24  once each 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf2 Earth}{\fs24\cf2 
\par \bullet }{\fs24  immunity to attacks from nonmagical weapons made of stone or metal 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  immunity to physical attacks by creatures of the elemental plane of Earth 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to breathe and move at full movement rate within the element of earth 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to cast }{\i\fs24 wall of stone}{\fs24  once 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf2 Fire}{\fs24\cf2 
\par \bullet }{\fs24  immunity to normal and magical fire 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  total protection from physical attacks by creatures of the elemental plane of Fire 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to breathe and move at full movement rate within the element of fire 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  total protection from hostile environmental effects while traveling plane of Fire 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to cast }{\i\fs24 wall of fire}{\fs24  once 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf2 Water}{\fs24\cf2 
\par \bullet }{\fs24  immunity to water- and cold-based attacks 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  total protection from physical attacks by creatures of the plane of Water 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to breathe and move at full movement rate within the element of water 
\par }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24  ability to cast }{\i\fs24 wall of ice}{\fs24  once 
\par 
\par \tab The auras do not restrict the caster in any way. He is free to move and act normally while under the influence of an aura. 
\par 
\par  
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Estate Transference}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Permanent
\par Casting Time: 10 turns
\par Area of Effect: 1,000 square feet/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This powerful sp
ell allows a caster to transfer a large area of land in the Prime Material plane to any of the elemental planes. All buildings, people, and wildlife within the area of effect are also transported. The land forms a pocket of the Prime Material plane within
 the elemental plane. The pocket is a sphere with a diameter equal to the diameter of the land. The surface of the pocket allows creatures to enter or exit the pocket, but prevents the elements from entering the pocket.
\par \tab Inside the pocket, the land is surrounded by air of a temperature matching that of the Prime Material plane at the moment the land was moved. In addition, a source of water is created within the pocket.
\par \tab Before the spell is cast, the area to be moved must be surrounded by solid markers of ma
terial from the destination plane. Thus, if a wizard  wants to move his castle to the Elemental Plane of Fire, he must first surround the area with solid blocks of matter from the Elemental Plane of Fire, such as hardened magma or magically-crystallized f
ire. The blocks must be spaced no more than five feet apart and may be placed above ground or under the surface (at a depth of no more than three feet).
\par \tab The wizard must be within the area to be moved when he casts the spell. When the land moves, a hemisphe
rical crater is left behind in the Prime Material plane. Inside its pocket on the desired plane, the land continues its existence as if nothing changed, with the exception of occasional visits from planar creatures.
\par \tab Any land that is moved in this manner can never again be moved with this spell.
\par \tab The material component (in addition to the markers) is the appropriate magical device to control elementals of the desired plane (}{\i\fs24 bowl commanding water elementals, brazier commanding fire elementals, censer controlling air elementals}{
\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 stone controlling earth elementals}{\fs24 
). The item must be permanently placed at the heart of the area of effect and cannot be used for any other purpose. If the device is disturbed in any way, the spell immediately fails, allowing the e
nergies of the elemental plane to flood into the protected area. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Glorious Transmutation}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Range: Touch
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Permanent
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell turns iron into silver or lead into gold at the caster's option. The prime ingredient for this spell is a magical item called the }{\i\fs24 philosopher's stone}{\fs24 
, which must be touched by the wizard and alchemically combined with the metal during casting. The formula for mixing the stone a
nd the metal must be known by the caster; this information is not provided by this spell and the spell is useless without it. (The exact ingredients and formula are decided by the Dungeon Master and must be discovered by the wizard in the course of advent
uring.)
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Philosopher's stones}{\fs24 
 vary in quality so much that each is capable of transmuting either 1d10x50 pounds of iron into an equal quantity of silver or 1d10x10 pounds of lead into the same amount of gold. It is not possible to know how much metal can be
 transmuted until the process is complete. If the caster has more iron or lead prepared than the spell is capable of changing, any excess is unchanged.
\par \tab The entire transmutation must be made at one time. Only one stone may be used per casting of the spell. The entire }{\i\fs24 philosopher's stone}{\fs24  is consumed in the process. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Stabilize*}{\b\fs24  (Abjuration) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S
\par Duration: 1d4+1 turns
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: 30-foot-radius circle
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell requires immense magical effort to cast, relegating it to the highest spell level. }{\i\fs24 Stabilize}{\fs24 
 negates the effects of wild magic regions, allowing the caster and all creatures in a 30-foot radius to cast spells and use magical items normally. The spell is centered on the caster and follows his movements.
\par \tab The caster's own spells never cause wild surges when cast within the duration of a }{\i\fs24 stabilize}{\fs24 
 spell, nor do the effects of wild surges extend into the protected area. Furthermore, the wild mage's spells function at his true level; Table 2 is }{\i\fs24 not}{\fs24  used to determine level variation. The spell affects }{\i\fs24 wildstrike, wildzone}
{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 wildwind}{\fs24 . 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Wail of the Banshee}{\b\fs24  (Necromancy) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Instantaneous
\par Casting Time: 9
\par Area of Effect: 30-foot-radius sphere
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab At the culmination of this dreadful spell, the wizard screams like a banshee (a groaning spirit). For each level of the caster, one listener within 30 feet hears the wail. Those who fail a saving throw vs. death magic die instantly.
\par \tab The wizard cannot
 be the victim of his own spell, nor can he choose who will be affected. If there are more potential victims than the level of the caster, the DM must randomly determine which creatures are affected. Creatures who cannot hear (due to ear plugs, deafness, 
etc.) can be targets, but cannot be affected and are considered to automatically make their saving throws.
\par \tab The material component is a lock of hair from an evil female elf. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Wildfire* (Invocation/Evocation) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 0
\par Component: V
\par Duration: Variable
\par Casting Time: 1
\par Area of Effect: Variable
\par Saving Throw: Variable
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab By means of this spell, the wild mage is able to channel raw magical energy through himself, shaping it into any form or effect he desires. The energy is similar in many ways to a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spell, but has unique differences.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Wildfire}{\fs24  allows the caster to create the effect of any wizard spell of 8th level or lower. He need only have general knowledge of the spell and its effects; the spell does not need to be in his own spellbooks.
\par \tab Any normal saving throws vs. the spell effects are made at a -2 penalty.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Wildfire}{\fs24  can also be used in the creation of magical items. The energy created by the spell may be used to generate effects that are not created by known spells.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Wildfire}{\fs24  can also be used to create ite
ms out of nothing. The magical energy can be shaped and hardened to form solid objects. These objects have a greenish, glowing tinge and radiate magic. These objects are stronger than steel yet possess almost no weight. They are immune to fire, cold, elec
tricity, and all forms of magical attack except }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spells. Even if they are subjected to these spells, a saving throw is allowed (equal to the creator's saving throw vs. spell).
\par \tab When creating objects, the caster is limited only by his ow
n skill and the dimensions of the object. Items larger than a 10-foot-radius sphere cannot be fashioned. Creating the object requires only one round, regardless of size. Thus, a wizard could make an impenetrable dome or a small boat with this spell.

\par \tab Objects made of }{\i\fs24 wildfire}{\fs24  are neither stable nor permanent. Since the object is made of magic separated from the magical continuum, the material gradually deteriorates until the magical bonds become too weak to hold the }{\i\fs24 
wildfire}{\fs24  in the chosen form. This decay takes 1d6+4 hours. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Wildwind*}{\b\fs24  (Conjuration/Summoning) }{\fs24 
\par Range: 100 yards
\par Components: V, S
\par Duration: 1d3 turns
\par Casting Time: 8
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell is similar in effect to }{\i\fs24 wildstrike}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 wildzone}{\fs24 . When cast, a wall of faint,
 multi-colored lights springs into existence at the point indicated by the caster. These lights form a line 150 feet long. After the first round of the spell, the wizard can move the wall of lights. Each round, the caster can move the wall in the same dir
ection or as much as 45 degrees to either side. Once the wall is set in motion, it cannot be stopped unless the spell is cancelled or dispelled. The lights can move 60 feet per round.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 wildwind}{\fs24  has two significant effects. First, all creatures struck by
 the magical lights suffer 2d6 points of damage. Second, any spellcaster struck while attempting to cast a spell automatically triggers a wild surge (use Table 2). Magical items that expend charges that touch the wall of lights automatically release one c
harge, also resulting in a wild surge. 
\par 
