\par \tab 
Once the spell is cast, the normal duration and area of effect for that spell are ignored. The focus begins to increase these factors of the spell's power. After one day, the amplified spell reaches its full area of effect. Thereafter, it remains over 
that area until the focus fails.
\par \tab The area affected by the focus (and its amplified spell) depends on the level of the caster. The spell expands in a radius from the focus, 20 feet per level of the caster, although it can deliberately be created smaller. W
ithin that area of effect, the amplified spell exerts its normal effect. A 13th-level priest could create a focus up to 260 feet in diameter.
\par \tab The material components are many, including special vestments, incense, oils, waters, and other equipment the DM 
deems appropriate. The cost of these materials is never less than 1,000 gp plus 100 gp per level of spell being amplified. These items are given up as offerings to the deity (perhaps to be distributed to the poor), and new ones must be obtained each time 
the spell is cast. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Fortify}{\b\fs24  (Necromancy) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Healing
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Special
\par Casting Time: 6
\par Area of Effect: Creature touched
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This is a simple cooperative magic spell. Only one priest can cast the spell, but like }{\i\fs24 mystic transfer}{\fs24 
, another priest is required for the spell to have any effect. Through this spell, the priest improves the quality of another priest's healing spells.
\par \tab For the }{\i\fs24 fortify}{\fs24  spell to work, it must be cast simultaneously with a }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds, cure serious wounds}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 cure critical wounds}{\fs24 . The priest casting }{\i\fs24 fortify}{\fs24 
 must lay his hand on the priest attempting the cure. When both spells are cast, additional energy flows through the second priest and into the creature being healed. }{\i\fs24 Fortify}{\fs24  auto
matically causes the cure spell to function at maximum effect. Thus, a }{\i\fs24 cure serious wounds}{\fs24  would automatically heal 17 points of damage and a }{\i\fs24 cure critical wounds}{\fs24  would heal 27 points of damage.
\par \tab The material component is the priest's holy symbol. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Genius}{\b\fs24  (Divination) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Thought
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Instantaneous
\par Casting Time: 5
\par Area of Effect: Caster
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell is similar to }{\i\fs24 idea}{\fs24 , except that the priest's player can ask the DM one question about any event occur
ring at the moment. The question must be somehow related to evaluation of the current situation, such as "What are these monsters?" Speculation about the future, such as "What's on the other side of the door?" is not permitted.
\par \tab As with }{\i\fs24 idea}{\fs24 , the DM must b
e careful in adjudicating this spell. The answer to the question should always be relevant and correct, although not necessarily complete, and should not be unbalancing to the situation. The answer can also be cryptic, in the form of a riddle or rhyme, de
pending on the DM's assessment of the situation. In general, the answer will be a single word or a short phrase of no more than five words.
\par \tab The material component is a gem of at least 50 gp value. This spell can be cast only once in any 12-hour period. Subsequent attempts to cast the spell result in no answer. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Inverted Ethics}{\b\fs24  (Enchantment/Charm) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Chaos
\par Range: 120 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 turn
\par Casting Time: 1
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell reverses the ethics o
f a person or group of people. While under the influence of this spell, a creature behaves in a manner opposite to the way he normally would behave. Thus, a shopkeeper influenced by inverted ethics will think it perfectly normal for someone to pick up an 
i
tem from his shop and walk out the door without paying for it. If someone tried to pay for an item, he would be insulted. If the spell is cast on a shopper in a store, he would find it natural to steal the item, thinking that he is behaving in a proper wa
y. If the spell is cast on a professed thief, he will no longer steal, choosing to pay for his goods instead.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Inverted ethics}{\fs24  does not cause a creature to actively commit evil deeds (or good deeds). Thus, an affected creature will not go on a shoplifting rampage; he will steal only as the opportunity presents itself.
\par \tab The spell affects one character per level of the caster within a 20' radius. Each target of the spell is allowed a saving throw vs. spell to avoid the effect.
\par \tab The material component is a miniature golden balance (i.e., similar to the scales of justice). 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Join With Astral Traveler}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Astral
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S
\par Duration: Special
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: The caster
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab When a priest casts the 7th-level }{\i\fs24 astral spell}{\fs24 , he leaves his physical body in suspended animation while his astral body travels. By touching the comatose body and casting }{\i\fs24 join with astral traveler}{\fs24 
, a priest can cause his own astral body to leave his physical body in suspended a
nimation. His astral body then travels along the silver cord of the originally projected priest. The caster joins the projected priest as if he were part of the original casting of the }{\i\fs24 astral spell}{\fs24 
; i.e., his own silver cord is connected to the priest's silver cord, and he is dependent upon the originally projected priest.
\par \tab A priest who casts the 7th-level }{\i\fs24 astral spell}{\fs24  can project as many as seven other creatures along with himself. However, priests casting }{\i\fs24 join with astral traveler}{\fs24  are an exception to this
 limit. Any number of priests may join another priest in the Astral plane by use of this spell.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Leadership}{\b\fs24  (Enchantment/Charm, Alteration) Reversible }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: War
\par Range: Special
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Special
\par Casting Time: Special
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell can be cast in one of two variations. The first, appropriate for battlefield use, has a range of 240 yards, duration of 1d4+6 turns, and a casting time of 1 turn. The priest can cast the spell on any single indiv
idual (a commander or hero) within his line of sight.
\par \tab While under the influence of this spell, the subject's command radius is increased by 50% (round fractions up).
\par \tab The reverse of this variation, }{\i\fs24 doubt}{\fs24 , requires the target to make a saving throw vs. spell. If failed, }{\i\fs24 doubt}{\fs24  halves the command radius (round fractions down) of the targeted individual for 1d3+4 turns.
\par \tab The material component for this variation is a pinch of steel dust.
\par \tab The second variation must take place in or within 100' of a place of wor
ship officially dedicated to the casting priest's deity. Both the priest and the individual to be affected must be present. The casting time is 5 turns and involves an intricate ritual and many prayers. At the conclusion of the spell, the subject's comman
d radius is doubled. This effect lasts 2d12 hours.
\par \tab The priest can cast either aspect (but not both at once) on himself. No individual can be the subject of more than one casting of this spell at one time, whether different aspects or cast by different prie
sts. If more than one spell is attempted on the individual, only the most recent casting takes effect. 
\par \tab The material component for the second variation is the priest's holy symbol. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Mental Domination}{\b\fs24  (Enchantment/Charm) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Thought
\par Range: 50 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 3 rounds/level
\par Casting Time: 4
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell is similar to the wizard spell }{\i\fs24 domination}{\fs24  in that it establishes a telepathic link between the priest and the subject through which the
 priest can control the subject's bodily movements. There are some significant differences between the spells, however.
\par \tab Elves and half-elves have no innate resistance to this spell. Priest and subject need not share a common language. The priest can force
 the subject into combat, but the subject's attack rolls suffer a -2 penalty. The priest cannot force the subject to cast spells or use any innate magical or magiclike abilities. The priest can force the subject to speak, although the priest cannot inject
 a full range of emotions into the subject's voice (everything said by the subject is in a monotone).
\par \tab This spell gives the priest no access to the subject's thoughts, memory, or sensory apparatus. Thus, the priest cannot see through the subject's eyes. To control the subject, the priest must be within the range of the spell}{\i\fs24  and}{\fs24 
 must be able to see the subject. Breaking either of these conditions causes the spell to terminate immediately.
\par \tab This spell requires a moderate level of concentration by the priest. 
While maintaining this spell, he can move or enter combat, but cannot cast another spell. If the priest is wounded, rendered unconscious, or killed, the spell immediately terminates.
\par \tab If the priest is 10th level or lower, he or she cannot force the subject
 to perform particularly delicate actions, such as picking a lock. At 11th level or higher, however, this restriction is removed. The priest could thus force a thief to pick a lock. Any such delicate actions suffer a -15% penalty (or -3 on 1d20) to reflec
t the "remote control" nature of the action.
\par \tab The material component is a mesh of fine threads that the priest loops around the fingertips of one hand and manipulates in the way that a puppeteer controls a puppet. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Modify Memory}{\b\fs24  (Enchantment/Charm) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Time
\par Range: 30 feet
\par Components: V, S
\par Duration: Permanent
\par Casting Time: Special
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell enables the caster to reach into the subject's mind and modify up to five minutes of his memory in one of the following ways:
\par \tab }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24 Eliminate all memory of an event the subject actually experienced. This spell cannot negate }{\i\fs24 charm, suggestion, geas, quest}{\fs24 , or similar spells.
\par \tab }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24 Allow the subject to recall with perfect clarity an event he actually experienced. For instance, he could recall every word from a five-minute conversation or every detail from a passage in a book.
\par \tab }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24 Change the details of an event the subject actually experienced.
\par \tab }{\fs24\cf2 \bullet }{\fs24 Implant a memory of an event the subject never experienced.
\par \tab Casting the spell takes o
ne round. If the subject fails to save vs. spell, the caster proceeds with the spell by spending up to five minutes visualizing the memory he wishes to modify in the subject. If the caster's concentration is disturbed before the visualization is complete,
 the spell is lost.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Modified memory}{\fs24  will not necessarily affect the subject's actions, particularly if they contradict his natural inclinations. An illogical }{\i\fs24 modified memory}{\fs24 
, such as the subject recalling how much he enjoyed drinking poison, will be dismissed by the subject as a bad dream or a memory muddied by too much wine. More useful applications of }{\i\fs24 modified memory}{\fs24 
 include implanting memories of friendly encounters with the caster (inclining the subject to act favorably toward the caster), changing the 
details of orders given to the subject by a superior, or causing the subject to forget that the caster cheated him in a card game. The DM reserves the right to decide whether a }{\i\fs24 modified memory}{\fs24 
 is too nonsensical to significantly affect the subject. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Probability Control}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Numbers
\par Range: 30 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Special
\par Casting Time: 4
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell allows the priest to increase or decrease by a small margin the probability of s
uccess for one action. This action can be anything that requires a die roll--an attack, a saving throw, an attempt to use thieving skills, an ability check, or even an attempt to successfully }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24 
 on target. The action must be something performed by a single creature.
\par \tab The basic modification is 15% (15 on 1d100 or 3 on 1d20), plus an additional 5% per five levels of the caster. This modification can be either positive or negative, as deemed by the spellcaster. Thus, a 10th-level priest can modify a subj
ect's saving throw or attack roll by +5 or -5, or a thief's "climb walls" roll by +25% or -25%. The priest may cast this spell on himself.
\par \tab For a noncombat action such as an attempt to climb a wall, the priest simply casts the spell on the subject immediat
ely before the action is attempted, informing the DM whether the modification is positive or negative. To use this spell in combat, the priest must specify the action to be affected (e.g., the target's next attack roll) and whether the modification will b
e positive or negative. The spell remains in effect until the subject attempts the specified action or until a number of rounds equal to the caster's level passes. If the latter occurs, the spell ends without effect.
\par \tab Once the spell is cast, the priest does not need to maintain any level of concentration; the spell will function even if the casting priest is killed before the spell takes effect.
\par \tab The subject of the spell has no way of knowing whether any modification made by this spell is positive or negativ
e (or even whether he was the subject of the spell at all). Thus, a lying priest could claim to raise a thief's chance of climbing the wall, while actually lowering it. The thief would be none the wiser. However, an unwilling subject of this spell receive
s a normal saving throw to negate its effect.
\par \tab The material components are a small cube of a thickened sugar-and-milk mixture and a cubic die of matching size. Both are consumed in the casting. 
\par  
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Rapport}{\b\fs24  (Divination, Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Thought
\par Range: 30 yards
\par Components: V, S
\par Duration: 1 turn+1 round/level
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell is a deeper and more intense version of }{\i\fs24 telepathy}{\fs24 . It allows the priest to communicate silently and instantly with a single willing subject. Participants may share deeper thoughts than with }{\i\fs24 telepathy}{\fs24 
, including emotions and memories. Each participant sees, hears, and otherwise senses everything experienced by the other, although such vicarious experiences feel diluted and cannot be mistaken for direct sensations.
\par \tab The participants can quickly share such personal concepts as plans, hopes, and fears, but they }{\i\fs24 cannot}{\fs24  share skills or spells. Thus, it is impossible to communicate the procedure for casting a particular spell or for picking a lock.

\par \tab Communication through }{\i\fs24 rapport}{\fs24  is approximately 15 times faster than verbal communication. As with }{\i\fs24 telepathy}{\fs24 
, the priest can establish separate "channels" to multiple individuals; each such linkage costs one casting of the spell. There is no "crosstalk" between the channels, however.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Rapport}{\fs24  cannot be used on unwilling subjects. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Solipsism}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Thought
\par Range: 10 yards/level
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Special
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: 100 sq. ft.+100 sq. ft./level
\par Saving Throw: Special
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This unusual spell is similar to }{\i\fs24 phantasmal force}{\fs24  and other illusion magic, except that the priest who casts the spell is the only creature who automatically believes the results of the spell. The spell creates the illusion
 of any object, creature, or force, as long as it is within the boundaries of the spell's area of effect. The illusion is visual and tactile (that is, it can be seen and felt), but no other sensory stimuli are created.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Solipsism}{\fs24  is the opposite of normal illusions in that anyone other than the caster must make an active effort to }{\i\fs24 believe}{\fs24 
 (rather than dis believe) the illusion. Characters trying to believe the reality of a solipsistic illusion must make a saving throw vs. breath weapon, modified by the magi
cal defense adjustment for Wisdom. A successful save means that the character believes the illusion and it is part of reality for him. A failed save means that the character cannot convince himself of the illusion's reality, and the illusion has no effect
 on him. A character can make a single attempt to believe each round.
\par \tab Unlike true illusions, the image created by this spell does more than just duplicate reality. The image formed is }{\i\fs24 real }{\fs24 for those who believe in it. The illusion has all the normal proper
ties that its form and function allow. Thus, a solipsistic bridge spanning a chasm could be crossed by the priest and those who believed. All others would see the priest apparently walking out onto nothingness. Likewise, a solipsistic giant would cause re
al damage to those who believed it.
\par \tab The illusion remains in effect for as long as the priest continues to concentrate on it, until the priest is struck in combat, or until he is rendered  unconscious. The level of concentration required is not extreme; the
 priest can move normally and may engage in combat, but is unable to cast any spell while maintaining a }{\i\fs24 solipsistic}{\fs24  illusion.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Solipsism}{\fs24  can create only illusions that are external to the priest. Thus, the priest cannot create an illusion that he is the size of a giant, is unwounded, or has sprouted wings.
\par \tab The material components are a lotus blossom that the priest must swallow and a bit of fleece. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Tanglefoot}{\b\fs24  (Alteration, Abjuration) Reversible }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: War
\par Range: 240 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 2 turns/level
\par Casting Time: 2 turns
\par Area of Effect: 100 sq. yards/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell temporarily doubles the movement cost of one region of ground. Units allied to the priest are unaffected and movement is made at normal cost; only enemy units suffer the penalty.
\par \tab A variety of effects result from the spell depending on the terrain: grass twists hinderingly around troops' ankles, swamp becomes more viscous, rocks and gravel shift underfoot, etc.
\par The spell affects only units--that is, groups of soldiers moving in regular or irregular formation. The spell does not affect individuals or monsters moving and operating alone. (When using the BATTLESYSTEM\'99
 rules, figures that represent individual heroes are not affected by this spell.)
\par \tab When casting this spell, the priest must have an uninterrupted line of sight to the terrain to be affected. The priest can choose the shape of the area, up to the maximum area of effect. This spell can create only one continuous area of }{\i\fs24 
tanglefoot}{\fs24 . There is no way of detecting that a particular area is under the influence of this spell simply by looking at the area. }{\i\fs24 Detect magic}{\fs24  will reveal that the area is magically affected.
\par \tab The reverse of this spell, }{\i\fs24 selective passage}{\fs24 , cuts the movement cost of an area in half (round fractions up) for friendly units. Again, individual heroes and creatures are not affecte
d by this spell (which means that advancing troops must be careful not to leave their leader behind!).
\par \tab The material component is a drop of molasses for tanglefoot, and a pinch of powdered graphite for selective passage.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Thought Broadcast}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Thought
\par Range: 30 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 turn+3 rounds/level
\par Casting Time: 5
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell turns the subject into a "thought broadcaster." For the duration of the spell, everyone within 30
 yards of the subject senses the subject's thoughts, making it impossible for him to lie, keep secrets, conceal motives, etc. The subject is not automatically aware that his thoughts are being sensed. Everyone who senses these thoughts, on the other hand,
 knows their source.
\par \tab This spell causes the broadcast of only surface thoughts and motivations, not memories. There is no need for a common language between broadcaster and receivers; for this purpose, thoughts are considered to be symbolic, not dependent o
n language. The detail level of the thoughts is insufficient for others to learn specific skills from the subject. Thus, if the subject casts a spell, everyone within range knows what spell is being cast before it takes effect, but no one learns any knowl
edge about how the spell is cast.
\par \tab If the broadcaster is }{\i\fs24 invisible}{\fs24  or hiding in shadows, the broadcast functions normally, and all receivers are aware that someone is in the vicinity whom they cannot see. While receivers cannot pinpoint the broadcaster's lo
cation, the broadcaster's thoughts will inevitably reveal his general position ("Oh no, he's looking right at me," etc.). A character hiding in shadows will be automatically detected, while attacks against an }{\i\fs24 invisible}{\fs24 
 broadcaster suffer a -2 penalty, rather than the normal -4. This spell totally negates the chance of surprise by the broadcaster.
\par \tab The subject must have an Intelligence score of 1 or more to become a broadcaster, and must have a "normal" mind as understood by PCs. Thoughts that are broadcast 
can be received only by individuals with Intelligence scores of 3 or better. An unwilling subject receives a normal saving throw vs. spell to avoid the effects. A willing subject can waive this saving throw.
\par \tab The material component is small balloon that the priest inflates upon casting. This balloon is consumed in the casting. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Tree Steed}{\b\fs24  (Alteration, Enchantment/Charm) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Travelers
\par Range: 10 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 hour/level
\par Casting Time: 4
\par Area of Effect: One log or plank
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell enchants a log, plank, or similar piece of wood to become a temporary steed. The log or plank must be at least one foot wide, three inches thick, and three to ten feet long. Any type of wood is suitable.
\par \tab When the spell is cast, the log sprouts four wooden, horselike legs. The }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  may be ridden like a normal horse and may be used to carry equipment. The }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24 
 can carry up to 600 pounds of riders and gear before breaking. If the }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  breaks under the weight of the riders or gear, the enchantment instantly ends and the }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24 
 again becomes a normal (although broken) log or plank.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  obeys all of the caster's verbal commands to move, slow, speed up, stop, and turn. It has a movement rate of 12 on land. It can move in the water (Sw 6), floating on the surface and paddling with its legs. The }{
\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  must remain within 10 yards of the caster in order to move; if the distance between the }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  and the caster exceeds 10 yards, the }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  stops until the caster is again within range.

\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24  will not fight for the caster and is incapable of any action other than movement. The }{\i\fs24 tree steed}{\fs24 
 does not become fatigued and does not eat. It has all the vulnerabilities of normal wood, including fire, and can be damaged by both magical and physical attacks. It has AC 8 and 20 hit points.
\par \tab The material components are a log or plank of suitable size and a horseshoe.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Uplift }{\b\fs24 (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: All
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, M
\par Duration: 1 turn
\par Casting Time: 12 hours
\par Area of Effect: One priest
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\i\fs24 
\par \tab Uplift}{\fs24  bestows increased spellcasting ability on one priest, including additional spells per level and use of spells beyond the caster's normal level. This cooperative spell requires two priests who mu
st spend the day casting this spell. During the casting, the priests must decide which additional spells (of all levels) are desired. Upon completion of the casting, the priests touch palms, and the priest of higher level receives a charge of magical ener
g
y. This charge temporarily boosts the level of the priest for spellcasting purposes. The amount of increase is one level per five levels of the lower level caster (fractions rounded up). If both priests are of equal level, the casters must decide who bene
fits from the spell.
\par \tab The spell grants the priest the spellcasting ability of the new level. It does not improve hit points, attack rolls, or other abilities. If the increase allows more spells per level, the additional spells are instantly placed in the ch
aracter's memory. A priest is also enabled to cast spells normally beyond his level. Range, duration, area of effect, and other variables are all based on the character's temporary level.
\par \tab The increased effect lasts only 1 turn. At the end of the turn, all additional spells are lost and the character reverts to his normal level.
\par \tab As an example, consider a party with a fallen comrade. The two priests in the party are 7th and 8th level, both unable to cast }{\i\fs24 raise dead}{\fs24 . After a night's rest, each priest adds }{\i\fs24 uplift}{\fs24 
 to his memorized spells. After casting the spell, the 8th-level priest suddenly gains the casting abilities of a 10th-level priest, including the ability to cast }{\i\fs24 raise dead}{\fs24 
. At the end of one turn, the priest's abilities revert to 8th-level.
\par \tab Casting 
this spell is an arduous task, causing a severe drain on the priests. When the spell expires, the uplifted character suffers 2d6 points of damage from mental exhaustion. This damage cannot be healed by any means until the character has had at least eight 
hours of rest.
\par \tab The material components are the priests' holy symbols and an offering worth at least 500 gp from each priest. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Weather Stasis}{\b\fs24  (Abjuration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Wards, Weather
\par Range: 30 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 hour/level
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: 10-foot cube/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\i\fs24 
\par \tab Weather stasis}{\fs24  maintains the weather conditions prevalent in the area of effect when the spell is cast. The spell affects a cube whose sides equal the caster's level times 10 feet (a 10th-level caster could affect a 100' x 100' x 100' cube).

\par \tab An area protected by }{\i\fs24 weather stasis}{\fs24  is unaffected by temperature variations in the surrounding environment. The spell also acts as a shield against rain, snow, and hail, which cannot enter the protected area. I
f conditions of precipitation existed in the area of effect when the spell was cast, the identical weather will continue for the duration of the spell.
\par \tab For example, }{\i\fs24 weather stasis}{\fs24  is cast in an area where the temperature is 75 F. and no precipitation is f
alling. Half an hour later, the temperature drops to 60 degrees and rain begins to fall. The protected area remains dry and the temperature stays at 75 degrees. If the spell had been cast while rain was falling in the area of effect, rain would continue t
o fall for the duration of the spell, even after it stopped raining in the surrounding area.
\par \tab All physical objects other than rain, snow, and hail can pass into the protected area. All creatures and characters can move freely into and out of the area. The s
pell does not prevent water-based spells or water-based creatures (such as water elementals) from operating in the area.
\par \tab The spell protects against both natural and magically generated weather. Night and day pass normally in the protected area, although temperature variations associated with night and day do not occur.
\par \tab The material components are the priest's holy symbol and a drop of rain. 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf2 Fifth-Level Spells }{\fs24 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Age Object }{\b\fs24 (Alteration) Reversible}{\b\fs24\cf2  }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Time
\par Range: 10 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Permanent
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: 1 cubic foot/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab With this spell, the caster can cause an amount of nonliving, nonmagical matter to age dramatically. Matter can be aged up to 20 years per level of the caster. The f
ollowing table gives typical results of 100 years of aging for various objects, arranged in order of descending severity: 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\nowidctlpar\tx4320\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {Object \tab Result of Aging 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx4320\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 diamond \tab none 
\par silver \tab becomes tarnished 
\par masonry \tab cracks and weakens 
\par iron \tab rusts and corrodes 
\par parchment \tab cracks, turns brittle 
\par wood \tab rots, crumbles, turns to sawdust 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab The caster controls the extent of the aging; thus, he could age a book so its pages become yellowed and brittle but stop short of causing the book to crumble to dust. As a guideline, each ad
ditional 100 years of aging causes an increasingly severe reaction. Thus, after 200 years, parchment might become little more than powder, while iron might begin to flake away at a touch.
\par \tab Many items (especially gems) show little reaction to age. The DM must adjudicate all effects.
\par \tab The material components are a flask of seawater and a piece of coal.
\par \tab The reverse of this spell, }{\i\fs24 youthful object}{\fs24 , returns an object ravaged by the effects of time to its original condition; thus, rusty iron becomes strong and sh
iny, crumbled masonry becomes firm, and rotten wood becomes solid. The age of matter can be reduced by 20 years per level of the caster.
\par \tab The material components for }{\i\fs24 youthful object}{\fs24  are a piece of eggshell and a hair from the head of a human or humanoid infant. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Barrier of Retention}{\b\fs24  (Abjuration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Wards
\par Range: Special
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 turn/level
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: 10'-cube/level
\par Saving Throw: Neg.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell creates a one-way invisible force field around the area of effect. The spell creates one 10' x 10' x 10' cube for every level of the caster. These can be arranged into any rectangular shape the caster desires.
\par \tab Intruders entering the protected area suffer no ill effects, but the }{\i\fs24 barrier of retention}{\fs24  prevents the
m from leaving. The spell affects all creatures who fail a saving throw vs. spell. The caster can pass in and out of the barrier freely.
\par \tab Intruders trapped by the }{\i\fs24 barrier of retention}{\fs24  can cast spells out of the barrier and can use spells such as }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24  t
o escape the protected area. Objects cannot be hurled out of the barrier but can be carried out by an escaping creature. }{\i\fs24 Dispel magic}{\fs24  and similar spells negate the }{\i\fs24 barrier}{\fs24 .
\par \tab The material component is a small cage made of silver wire. The caster must walk around the perimeter of the area of effect when casting. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Blessed Abundance}{\b\fs24  (Conjuration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Creation
\par Range: Touch
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Permanent
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: 1 cubic foot/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell allows
 a priest to duplicate a specified amount of animal or vegetable matter. Magical items and minerals (including rocks, metals, and gemstones) cannot be duplicated. Although organic materials (such as food or living plants) can be duplicated, living creatur
es cannot be copied by this spell.
\par \tab The caster can create 1 cubic foot of material per his experience level. The material to be duplicated must be equal to or less than 1 cubic foot in size or volume. For example, a 9th-level priest can create up to 9 cubic
 feet of animal or vegetable matter. Using a loaf of bread 1 cubic foot in size, he can produce nine such loaves; using a bucket of apples totaling 1 cubic foot in volume, he can create nine such buckets.
\par \tab The material component is the priest's holy symbol. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Champion's Strength}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Law
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Special
\par Casting Time: 2
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\i\fs24 
\par \tab Champion's strength}{\fs24  bestows one member of a group with attack and damage bonuses from the rest of the group. The recipient of the spell can then fight as the group's champion.
\par \tab The spell draws bonuses from one person for every two levels of the priest. All characters involved must be within a 30'-radius of the priest. At the time of casting, the priest
 designates the recipient of the spell and the contributors. All characters who contribute to the spell must do so willingly.
\par \tab When the spell is completed, the designated character (the group's champion) gains any non-magical bonuses to THAC0 and damage po
ssessed by the characters who contributed to the spell. Characters without bonuses or with combat penalties could conceivably be included in the spell; such characters count against the maximum number of creatures that can be affected. Penalties are likew
ise applied to the champion; contributors to this spell must be chosen carefully.
\par \tab The bonuses gained through this spell are added to the character's own bonuses (if any). The champion channels the energy of others through himself, improving his fighting ability.
\par \tab The champion must be in the line of sight and within 30 feet of the characters aiding him. Characters who contribute their bonuses must concentrate on the champion for the duration of the spell. If this concentration is broken (by moving more than 
10 feet per round, fighting, being struck, or losing sight of the champion), that character's contribution is immediately lost.
\par \tab The spell expires when the last character contributing power to the champion ceases concentration.
\par \tab A champion may benefit from only one }{\i\fs24 champion's strength}{\fs24  spell at one time. Contributors can aid only one champion at one time.
\par \tab The material component is a chain of five gold links worth at least 1,000 gp. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Chaotic Commands}{\b\fs24  (Enchantment/Charm) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Chaos
\par Range: Touch
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 turn/level
\par Casting Time: 3
\par Area of Effect: One creature
\par Saving Throw: Special
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\i\fs24 
\par \tab Chaotic commands}{\fs24  renders a creature immune to magical commands. }{\i\fs24 Taunt, forget, suggestion, domination, geas, demand, succor, command, enthrall, quest, exaction}{\fs24 
, and other spells that place a direct verbal command upon a single individual automatically fail.
\par \tab In addition, anyone casting one of these spells on a creature protected by }{\i\fs24 chaotic commands}{\fs24  must save vs. spell. Failure means that the caster must obey his own magic; the spell's effect has backfired on the caster.
\par \tab The material component is a piece of eelskin. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Clear Path}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) Reversible }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Travelers
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 hour/level
\par Casting Time: 5
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell clears away weeds, stones, and other debris in a 10-foot-wide path extending 10 feet in front of the caster. The caster can create a continuous path for the duration of the spell, clearing a 10-foot-square ahead of him 
as long as he continues to move forward. The spell affects jungles, forests, rocky ground, and snow.
\par \tab The result of the cleared path is that movement costs are reduced by half. This is reflected in a reduction of the penalty against movement in rough terrain. (See Table 74 of the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master's Guide }{\fs24 
for terrain costs for movement.) For example, if }{\i\fs24 clear path}{\fs24  is used in heavy jungle, the movement cost is reduced from 8 to 4. In no case can }{\i\fs24 clear path}{\fs24  reduce movement cost below 1.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Clear path}{\fs24  has no effect on rivers, lakes, or other bodies of water, nor does it affect quicksand, lava, or similar natural obstacles. It also has no effect on magically-created terrain or manmade barricades.
\par \tab A priest using the }{\i\fs24 clear path}{\fs24  spell can be tracked easily. Tracking proficiency is not required.
\par \tab The material components are a knife blade and a straw from a broom.
\par \tab The reverse, }{\i\fs24 clutter path}{\fs24 , causes weeds, small stones, and similar debris to litter a 10-foot path extending 10 feet behind the caster. This hides a trail, mak
ing tracking more difficult. The caster can create a continuous path for the duration of the spell. The chance to successfully track on a cluttered path is reduced by 50%.
\par \tab The material components are a handful of pebbles and a handful of weeds. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Cloud of Purification}{\b\fs24  (Evocation) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Elemental Air, Water
\par Range: 30 yards
\par Components: V, S
\par Duration: 1 round/level
\par Casting Time: 5
\par Area of Effect: 20-foot cube
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell creates a billowy cloud of magical vapors that moves in the direct
ion of the prevailing wind at a rate of 20 feet per round. A strong wind (greater than 15 miles per hour) breaks it up in 4 rounds, and a greater wind (25 MPH or more) prevents the use of the spell. Thick vegetation disperses the cloud in 2 rounds.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 cloud of purification}{\fs24 
 transmutes organic filth, garbage, and vermin (mice, rats, rot grubs, and so on) into an equal quantity of pure water. For example, a nest of rot grubs caught in the cloud would "melt," becoming small puddles of clean water. If the spel
l is cast over a body of water, the cloud merges with a portion of the water equal to its own size, transmuting any filth, microbes, small fish, or other "impurities" into clean water.
\par \tab The cloud's vapors are heavier than air, so they sink to the lowest level of the land (even down holes in the ground). Thus, this spell is perfect for cleansing a sewer or well.
\par \tab This spell in no way affects magical creatures or creatures larger than a normal rat. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Consequence}{\b\fs24  (Divination) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Numbers, Divination
\par Range: 0
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Instantaneous
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell allows the priest to determine how one recent event fits into the "grand scheme." By casting this spell, the priest can determine 
whether the sequence or situation that gave rise to the specific event is complete or whether it is ongoing; whether it was a significant or insignificant event in the larger picture; or whether it will continue to have repercussions for the participants.

\par \tab 
Using his knowledge of circumstances, the DM communicates these facts to the caster's player. This "arcane message" is normally straightforward and easy to understand, but in the case of highly complex circumstances, the message might be cryptic. In any c
ase, the message will always be truthful.
\par \tab As an example, consider a priest and his party who are on a holy quest to retrieve an item of power. On the way to the location of this item, the party is ambushed by evil creatures from the Inner Planes but manag
es to defeat them. Concerned that these creatures might be outlying guards protecting the item of interest, the priest casts }{\i\fs24 consequence}{\fs24 
, hoping for guidance. The DM knows that these creatures have nothing to do with the quest; the encounter was coincident
al. However, the surviving monsters will soon be returning with reinforcements to avenge their dead. Therefore, the DM tells the priest's player, "To your goals these have no place, but still they can cause more woe."
\par \tab Casting this spell "taints" subsequen
t castings of the same spell within a 24-hour span. A second attempt within this period always results in the same message as the first, regardless of the true situation. If a second priest casts the spell within 24 hours of another casting, he receives a
n accurate reading.
\par \tab The material component is three special coins or dice made of platinum (total value of at least 1,000 gp), which the priest tosses in his hand while concentrating on the spell. The coins or dice are not consumed in the casting. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Disguise}{\b\fs24  (Illusion/Phantasm) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: War
\par Range: 200 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 turn/3 levels
\par Casting Time: 2 turns
\par Area of Effect: One unit up to 300 individuals
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell changes the appearance of a single unit so it resembles another unit. The }{\i\fs24 disguise}{\fs24 
 can cause the affected creatures to appear to be of another class, nationality, rank, race, alignment, or military affiliation (i.e., a unit from one army may appear wearing the armor and carrying the colors of another army). }{\i\fs24 Disguise}{\fs24 
 cannot change the size category of the unit's members. Thus, a unit of humans may appear to be a unit of elves, but may not appear as a unit of giants or halflings. The spell does not affect the size of the overall unit; a unit of 50 creatures will s
till appear to be a unit of 50 creatures.
\par \tab The disguised unit may appear to be carrying any melee or personal missile weapons (e.g., axes, long swords, crossbows, etc.), and may appear to be wearing any type of armor. In combat, however, the unit attacks a
nd defends with its real weapons and armor regardless of the gear they may appear to be carrying.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Disguise}{\fs24  is most effective at long range. If another unit moves within 20 yards of a disguised unit, it automatically sees through the illusion.
\par \tab The caster automatically sees through the illusion. Members of the subject unit see no change in their appearance. }{\i\fs24 True seeing}{\fs24 
 or similar magic is required for other individuals to see through the disguise (unless they move within 20 yards of the unit).
\par \tab The material components are a fine silk veil and a length of woven platinum wire. The wire is consumed during the casting. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Easy March}{\b\fs24  (Invocation) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Travelers
\par Range: 50 feet
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 day/level
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: One creature/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell enables a number of creatures equal to the caster's level to force march for a number of days equal to the caster's level. Creatures affected by }{\i\fs24 easy march}{\fs24  can travel 2 \'bd times their normal movement rate without
 any risk of fatigue; thus, they are not required to make a Constitution check at the end of the day.
\par \tab All creatures affected by this spell suffer a -1 penalty to their attack rolls for the duration of the spell; this modifier is not cumulative (that is, a party experiencing its second day of }{\i\fs24 easy march}{\fs24 
 suffers only a -1 penalty). The modifier cannot be negated by resting. 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Easy march}{\fs24  has no effect on modifiers to movement due to terrain, fatigue, weather, or other normal factors. (Refer to Chapter 14 of the }{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook}{\fs24  for more about force marching.)
\par \tab The material component is a piece of shoe leather. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Elemental Forbiddance}{\b\fs24  (Abjuration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Wards, Elemental--Air, Earth, Fire, Water
\par Range: Special
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1 turn/level
\par Casting Time: 1 turn
\par Area of Effect: 5'-cube/level
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell prevents the entry of all elementals into the area of effect. Further, elementals outside the area of effect cannot make physical attacks against those inside. Spells and missile attacks can be cast into the area by elementals.
\par \tab The spell affects a cube whose sides equal the caster's level times 5 feet (a 12th-level priest could affect an area equal to a 60' x 60'x 60' cube).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Elemental forbiddance}{\fs24  has no effect on elementals that are within the area of effect when the spell is cast. If such elementals leave the area of effect, they cannot reenter.
\par \tab The material components are the priest's holy symbol and four glass beads, each of a different color (green, blue, red, and yellow). The priest must pace out the perimeter of the warded area at the time of casting. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Extradimensional Manipulation}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Numbers
\par Range: 10 yards
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 2d12 rounds+4 rounds/level
\par Casting Time: 5
\par Area of Effect: One extradimensional space up to
\par 20 feet x 20 feet
\par Saving Throw: Special
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell allows the priest to alter the characteristics of certain extradimensional spaces such as those created by rope trick and similar spells or those contained in items like }{\i\fs24 bags of holding}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 portable holes}{\fs24 
. 
\par }{\i\fs24 Extradimensional manipulation}{\fs24  can increase or reduce the size of a single extradimensional space. The amount of increase or decrease depends on the level of the caster:
\par  
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\nowidctlpar\tx2880\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b { Level \tab Multiplier 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2880\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24  Up to 10 \tab     x2 
\par  11 to 16 \tab     x3 
\par  17 or above \tab     x4 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This means that a 10th-level priest can double the capacity of a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  or decrease it to half its normal size. A 15th-level priest can triple the capacity or reduce it to one-third capacity.
\par \tab If the size and capacity of an
 extradimensional space is decreased, any contents of the space that exceed the current capacity are expelled (determined randomly). These contents are expelled from the space in the same way they originally entered it, if that path is still open. If the 
path is closed, as it would be if a bag of holding were tied shut or a portable hole were folded up, the "extra" contents are expelled into the Astral plane. Any items in an enlarged space when the spell duration expires suffer the same fate.
\par \tab Placing an extradimensional space inside another such space, such as placing a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  inside a }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24  (see the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master's Guide}{\fs24 ), is a dangerous undertaking. }{\i\fs24 
Extradimensional manipulation}{\fs24  may be cast for the purpose of removing this danger. When us
ed in this manner, the size of the space cannot be affected. However, while this version is in effect, the affected extradimensional space can be placed within another such space (or another extradimensional space may be placed within the affected space) 
with no adverse consequences. If one space is within the other when the spell expires, the usual consequences ensue immediately.
\par \tab If the space to be affected is being maintained by a spellcaster, as in the case of a }{\i\fs24 rope trick,}{\fs24  that spellcaster receives a s
aving throw to resist the manipulation. If the space is created by a magical item, however, no saving throw is allowed.
\par \tab The material component is a strip of gold tissue worth at least 5 gp that is twisted into a Moebius strip. The strip is consumed in the casting. 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf2 Extradimensional Pocket}{\b\fs24  (Alteration) }{\fs24 
\par Sphere: Numbers
\par Range: Touch
\par Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: 1d12 rounds+2 turns/level
\par Casting Time: 1 round
\par Area of Effect: Special
\par Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell allows the priest to create a single extradimensional space or pocket like the one inside  a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24 
. The spell must be cast on a container such as a sack, bag, or backpack. Once under the influence of the spell, the container opens into a nondimensional space and is much larger inside than 
its outside dimensions. The container always weighs a fixed amount, regardless of what is put inside. This weight and the capacity of the extradimensional space depend on the level of the caster:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\nowidctlpar\tx1440\tx3240\tx5040\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {  \tab Apparent \tab Weight \tab Volume 
